      <h1 id="core-cluster-upgrades-z-stream" class="group scroll-mt-20">Complete a z-stream cluster update with {"{"}{"{"} cgu_operator {"}"}{"}"}<a href="#core-cluster-upgrades-z-stream" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p>You can update clusters to z-stream patch releases by using RHACM policies and Topology Aware Lifecycle Manager (TALM).</p>
      <h2 id="core-cluster-upgrade-z-stream_core-cluster-upgrades-z-stream" class="group scroll-mt-20">Updating clusters with z-stream releases<a href="#core-cluster-upgrade-z-stream_core-cluster-upgrades-z-stream" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can update clusters to z-stream patch releases by using RHACM policies and Topology Aware Lifecycle Manager (TALM). Z-stream updates apply security fixes and bug fixes with minimal risk and should be performed regularly to maintain cluster security.</p>
      <p>Apply z-stream updates as soon as they become available to address critical vulnerabilities and maintain security compliance.</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The following procedure uses <code>spoke1</code> and <code>spoke2</code> as example cluster names. Replace these with your actual cluster names throughout.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have completed the pre-update health check successfully.</li>
      <li>TALM is installed and configured on the Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management (RHACM) hub cluster.</li>
      <li>A z-stream patch release is available on the Red Hat Customer Portal.</li>
      <li>A recent etcd backup exists that was created within the past 24 hours.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify the current cluster version by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get clusterversion"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following example shows the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME      VERSION   AVAILABLE   PROGRESSING   SINCE   STATUS\nversion   4.20.0    True        False         30d     Cluster version is 4.20.0"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check for available z-stream updates by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc adm upgrade"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following example shows the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Cluster version is 4.20.0\n\nUpstream is unset, so the cluster will use an appropriate default.\nChannel: stable-4.20 (available channels: candidate-4.20, eus-4.20, fast-4.20, stable-4.20)\n\nRecommended updates:\n\n  VERSION     IMAGE\n  4.20.1      quay.io/openshift-release-dev/ocp-release@sha256:abc123...\n  4.20.2      quay.io/openshift-release-dev/ocp-release@sha256:def456..."} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Review the OpenShift Container Platform release notes for the target version to identify security fixes and bug fixes included in the z-stream release.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a RHACM policy for the z-stream update:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\nkind: Policy\nmetadata:\n  name: core-upgrade-4.20.1-policy\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  remediationAction: inform\n  disabled: false\n  policy-templates:\n  - objectDefinition:\n      apiVersion: policy.open-cluster-management.io/v1\n      kind: ConfigurationPolicy\n      metadata:\n        name: upgrade-cluster-version\n      spec:\n        remediationAction: inform\n        severity: high\n        object-templates:\n        - complianceType: musthave\n          objectDefinition:\n            apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\n            kind: ClusterVersion\n            metadata:\n              name: version\n            spec:\n              channel: stable-4.20\n              desiredUpdate:\n                version: 4.20.1"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>Replace <code>4.20.1</code> with your target z-stream version. The policy is set to <code>inform</code> so it does not immediately push the update. The <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> custom resource (CR) enforces the policy at the scheduled update time.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the policy to the RHACM hub cluster by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f core-upgrade-4.20.1-policy.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify the policy and placement resources were created by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get policy core-upgrade-4.20.1-policy -n openshift-upgrade-policies\n$ oc get placementbinding -n openshift-upgrade-policies\n$ oc get placement -n openshift-upgrade-policies"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following example shows the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                           REMEDIATION ACTION   COMPLIANCE STATE   AGE\ncore-upgrade-4.20.1-policy    inform               NonCompliant       30s"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If no <code>PlacementBinding</code> or <code>Placement</code> exists, the policy will not apply to any clusters. Create appropriate placement resources for your target clusters.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR for the z-stream update:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ran.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ClusterGroupUpgrade\nmetadata:\n  name: core-zstream-upgrade-4.20.1\n  namespace: openshift-upgrade-policies\nspec:\n  clusters:\n  - spoke1\n  - spoke2\n  managedPolicies:\n  - core-upgrade-4.20.1-policy\n  enable: false\n  remediationStrategy:\n    maxConcurrency: 2\n    timeout: 60"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>clusters</code>: Specifies your target cluster names.</li>
      <li><code>maxConcurrency</code>: Specifies the number of clusters to update simultaneously.</li>
      <li><code>timeout</code>: Specifies the maximum time in minutes to wait for each cluster update. For example, 60 equals 1 hour per cluster.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply and enable the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR to start the update by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f core-zstream-cgu.yaml\n$ oc patch cgu core-zstream-upgrade-4.20.1 \\\n  -n openshift-upgrade-policies \\\n  --type merge \\\n  -p '{\"spec\":{\"enable\":true}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Monitor the update progress by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get cgu core-zstream-upgrade-4.20.1 -n openshift-upgrade-policies -w\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get clusterversion -w\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get co"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR progresses through these states:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>Preparing</code>: TALM validates cluster readiness</li>
      <li><code>InProgress</code>: Clusters are updating</li>
      <li><code>Complete</code>: All clusters successfully updated</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Operators update sequentially during the z-stream update.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify the cluster version and Operator health by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get clusterversion -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.desired.version}'\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get co"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The cluster version must show the target z-stream version. All Operators must show <code>AVAILABLE=True</code>, <code>PROGRESSING=False</code>, <code>DEGRADED=False</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check workload health by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get pods -A | grep -v Running | grep -v Completed"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Pods must not show unexpected failures or crash loops.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify CNF-specific resources by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get performanceprofile\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get ptpconfig -n openshift-ptp\n$ oc --context=spoke1 get sriovnetworknodepolicy -n openshift-sriov-network-operator"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>All custom resources must show expected status.</p>
      </li>
      <li>Run smoke tests for critical workloads to ensure functionality after the update.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Troubleshooting</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      If the <code>ClusterGroupUpgrade</code> CR is stuck in <code>Preparing</code> state, check policy compliance in RHACM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get policy core-upgrade-4.20.1-policy -n openshift-upgrade-policies"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      If a cluster Operator becomes degraded during the update, check Operator logs by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 logs -n openshift-<operator_namespace> deployment/<operator_deployment>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      If the update exceeds the timeout, investigate slow nodes or image pull issues by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --context=spoke1 get events -A --sort-by='.lastTimestamp' | tail -20"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ul>
